.TH APTQ 1 "2026-11-25" "aptfz 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
aptq \- non\-interactive query into the installed apt packages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aptq
[OPTIONS]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aptq
is the scriptable counterpart to
.BR aptfz (1).
It shares the exact same query engine and package cache and prints matching
package names, one per line, with no picker, no colors and no extra prose.
With
.B \-t
it prints only the total installed size.
.PP
Because its output is meant for pipes (\fBgrep\fR(1), \fBxargs\fR(1), ...),
.B aptq
never prints anything but results on standard output. Errors go to standard
error and set a non\-zero exit status.
.PP
Package names are the names apt accepts: the plain name for packages of the
native architecture and for architecture\-independent packages, and
\fIname\fR:\fIarch\fR for packages of a foreign (multiarch) architecture.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Filter options
Filters combine with AND.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-explicit
Only manually installed packages (\fBapt\-mark showmanual\fR).
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-deps
Only automatically installed packages, i.e. dependencies (\fBapt\-mark showauto\fR).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-notadep
Only packages that no installed package requires (no installed package lists
them in Depends or Pre\-Depends).
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-notoptdep
Only packages that no installed package recommends or suggests.
.TP
.B \-\-has\-note
Only packages that have a note (see
.BR aptfz (1)).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-note
Only packages without a note.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-field " \fIFIELD VALUE\fR"
Only packages whose \fIFIELD\fR matches \fIVALUE\fR. Matching is a
case\-insensitive substring match unless \fB\-x\fR is given. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-any " \fIVALUE\fR"
Only packages where any field matches \fIVALUE\fR. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-exact\-match
Use exact equality instead of substring matching for \fB\-f\fR and \fB\-a\fR.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-invert " [\fIFILTER\fR]"
Bare \fB\-v\fR inverts the entire result of all other filters and searches,
as one unit. \fB\-v\fR \fIFILTER\fR (repeatable) inverts only that one canned
filter and leaves the others untouched. \fIFILTER\fR is one of
\fBexplicit\fR, \fBdeps\fR, \fBnotadep\fR, \fBnotoptdep\fR, or the letters
\fBe\fR, \fBd\fR, \fBn\fR, \fBk\fR. Repeated \fB\-v FILTER\fR options are
combined as NOT(A AND B). The note filters are never inverted.
.SS Sort options
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sort\-by " [\fIFIELD\fR]"
Sort by \fIFIELD\fR. The default is Name; a bare \fB\-s\fR means
Installed_Size. \fB\-\-size\fR is accepted as a synonym.
.TP
.BR \-\-asc ", " \-\-desc
Sort ascending or descending. Size defaults to descending, everything else
to ascending.
.SS Size
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-totalsize
Print the total installed size of the (filtered) packages instead of names.
.SS Other
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH FIELDS
Names accepted by \fB\-f\fR and \fB\-s\fR (case\-insensitive; space, \fB\-\fR
and \fB_\fR are equivalent; \fBpackage\fR, \fBdepends\fR and \fBsize\fR are
accepted as aliases for Name, Depends_On and Installed_Size):
.PP
Name, Version, Architecture, Description, Installed_Size (bytes),
Install_Reason, Section, Priority, Maintainer, Homepage, Source, Essential,
Multi_Arch, Depends_On, Pre_Depends, Recommends, Suggests, Conflicts, Breaks,
Provides, Replaces, Required_By, Recommended_By, Suggested_By.
.PP
The last three are the reverse of Depends/Pre\-Depends, Recommends and
Suggests, computed over the installed packages. To find packages with an
empty field use an exact empty match, e.g.
.B \-x \-f Required_By ""
(this is what \fB\-n\fR does).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
aptq \-e                       # manually installed packages
aptq \-e \-n                    # ... that nothing requires
aptq \-e \-n \-k \-s \-\-desc      # ... and nothing recommends or suggests, biggest first
aptq \-d \-t                    # total size of dependency packages
aptq \-f Section games         # packages in a section containing "games"
aptq \-e \-v notadep \-v notoptdep
                             # manual packages that something relies on
aptq \-n \-k | xargs apt show  # pipe names into other tools
.fi
.SH CACHE
The package list is cached in
.IR $XDG_CACHE_HOME/aptfz/packages.json
(default \fI~/.cache/aptfz/packages.json\fR; override with
.BR APTFZ_CACHE_PATH ).
The cache validates itself on every run against the modification time, size
and inode of \fI/var/lib/dpkg/status\fR and \fI/var/lib/apt/extended_states\fR
and is rebuilt automatically when either changed, so there is no refresh
command and it cannot be stale. Rebuilding takes a fraction of a second.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on an error (bad field or filter name, dpkg unavailable),
2 on an invalid command line.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR aptfz (1),
.BR apt\-mark (8),
.BR dpkg\-query (1)
